@*
    The Rows tab's slot. ObjectDetail renders this only when the service said the relation's rows may be
    read (DatabaseRelationSummary.Rows.Allowed); every refusal - Marten's own tables, a foreign table, a view
    over a hidden type, a gate that is shut - is drawn by the page before it gets here, and the row service
    refuses again on its own if anything changed in between.

    A component with a fixed signature rather than markup inline in the page, so the page does not change
    when what is behind it does. The parameters below are the whole contract between the two:

      Schema  - the relation's schema, as the catalog spells it
      Name    - the relation's name, likewise
      Detail  - the DatabaseObjectDetail the page already read (columns, row key, keys both ways), typed
                object for the reason DocumentComponentModel gives; its foreign keys are what a cell follows
      Ready   - whether the tab may read: false while the page is still applying the scope its address
                names, or reading the next object, so rows are never read for an address and a scope that
                are not the object's on screen
*@

<div class="ms-db-rows-slot" data-schema="@Schema" data-name="@Name">
    <TableRowsTab Schema="@Schema" Name="@Name" Detail="Detail" Ready="Ready" />
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The relation's schema.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Schema { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The relation's name.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The relation's <c>DatabaseObjectDetail</c>. Typed <see cref="object" /> - see <c>DocumentComponentModel</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public object? Detail { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the tab may read now; see <c>TableRowsTab.Ready</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Ready { get; set; } = true;
}
